# THE BYTES A CLONE SEES ARE THE BYTES THIS ROOT'S CHECKS DESCRIBE.
#
# `contracts/shape-pin.yaml` records a sha256 of every shape file copied here,
# and `scripts/validate-pins.py` recomputes each one from the bytes on disk.
# Git for Windows' installer default is `core.autocrlf=true`; a clone made under
# it writes CRLF into the worktree, and `make validate` then goes red on every
# pinned file for a colleague who did nothing but clone this repository
# (openRepoShape #51, ruled by Brett Heap on 2026-09-05; the shape's own
# statement of the rule is `shape/.gitattributes`, pinned and left in place).
#
# Before the split into three repositories this file carried the same rule for
# a second reason: `openRepoTools --install` and its tests compare installed
# copies with the checkout BYTE FOR BYTE. Those files are the code leg's now,
# and a superproject's attributes never reach into a submodule's worktree, so
# that statement moved with them, word for word, to `code/.gitattributes`; the
# documents in `spec/` carry their own copy of the rule too.
#
# NO PATH IS LISTED BY NAME in the rule below: every file here is text, so
# `text=auto` leaves binary detection with git. The block after the rule is
# the split's (openRepoShape #471), and it stays last: the later line wins.

* text=auto eol=lf
# openRepoShape (#471): the pinned shape copies below check out with LF everywhere, as contracts/shape-pin.yaml records their bytes
/scripts/validate-pins.py text eol=lf
/scripts/validate-manifest.py text eol=lf
/scripts/bootstrap.py text eol=lf
/Makefile text eol=lf
/.github/workflows/validate.yml text eol=lf
/AGENTS-shape.md text eol=lf
/scripts/repo_shape.py text eol=lf
/scripts/validate-repository-naming.py text eol=lf
/contracts/repository-naming.yaml text eol=lf
